 and it's not only for  the for the veteran he or she it's also  for their family  you know I've I've often said that um  one of my true regrets is that  you know when when I came back I was a  worker bee I mean I  I put in some hours  and uh that continued long  but um  one of my regrets has always been that I  wasn't around my kids as much as I would  have liked to be  but given the PTSD and how I was then  probably a good thing I wasn't around  and  you know I got my PTSD was pretty  manageable now I have a good  relationship with each one of them the  one thing about about PTSD to understand  that how how it is is understand how I  am after I've had it treated I talked to  my wife about the  PTSD and and um  what I'd like to do to  get rid of it I mean it just to get back  to the way I was and I told her I'd like  to try psychotage because I've never  done anything like that  um and and uh so  she had me she hooked had me hooked up  with two people that treat vets under  the radar within two weeks  and I met with them later that year in  August in Hawaii started doing it on the  first day I took ayahuasca  and um  I I didn't um  didn't hallucinate but I could tell and  what the psychedelics do the  psychedelics is make the person that's  being treated receptive to the treatment  it makes them willing to make changes  open to make changes and more open to  talk about and reliving the experiences  that got them there  so Ayahuasca did that for me on the  second day I took  magic mushrooms  and my guide told me he said I made a  teapot of of tea with these magic  mushrooms with psilocybin and I made it  really strong it holds three cups you'll  only need one cup  well I drank all three cups  and I ate the tea bags  the guy said I've never seen anything  like this before  and did I hallucinate no but um was I  lit I'd say righteously and um  of course and we stepped through it more  and it's painful and tears and  they're getting treated with  psychedelics is not a 